          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is G47 a code, a category or a block, and how would I tell the difference?",
          "answer": "I could not find anything about G47 in the material I searched. The results covered I10 (hypertension), J44 (COPD), J45 (asthma) and K21 (reflux), but none of them mention G47. So I cannot tell you from these sources whether G47 is a code, a category or a block.\n\nWhat the results do explain about the general structure:\n\n- **Code:** The most specific entry. Some codes are complete as they are. The hypertension document says I10 \"is a complete code at three characters\" and \"takes none\" of the extra characters (coding-hypertension-i10).\n- **Category:** A group of related codes. Most categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is complete. The asthma and COPD documents describe this pattern (coding-asthma-j45, coding-copd-j44).\n- **Block:** A range of codes covering a family of conditions. The hypertension document places I10 in \"the hypertensive-disease block that runs from I10 to I16\" (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\nThe results also noted that ICD-10-CM (the US version) and WHO's ICD-10 differ, and that codes can change between years. So a code's status depends on which version and which year you are using (coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10).\n\nTo check G47 itself, look it up in the current official ICD-10-CM tabular list from the CDC/NCHS, or ask a medical coder. Those sources will show whether G47 has more characters beneath it and where it sits in the chapter structure.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
